A||||8||ICQ196344||INOPERATIVE SPEEDOMETER
Z||000000||ICQ196344||05-28-99||03:07 PM||||My '84 fiero speedometer does not work. I tried substituting a sender and a gauge, but still no luck. Neither the speedometer of the odometer work.What is the most effective way to issolate the problem? 
Z||000001||PaUL||05-28-99||04:25 PM||||See if the vehicle speed sensor is actually sending pulses to the speedo. The Haynes manual has the color codes for the wires, find the right wire, attach some kind of voltmeter and drive. If no pulses detect then it must be in the wire, if there are pulses then the prob is in the speedo itself. Also check the connectors going to dashboard. These are kind of flimsy and can come loose. I mean the ones that plug into the white plastic housing with the flexible printed circuit.
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||05-29-99||02:23 AM||||The connector that has the speedometer/odometer signals is the edge-connector at the bottom of your gauge unit. Check this connector for oxidation and try to reseat it a few times.
Z||000003||ICQ196344||05-29-99||08:35 PM||||I am getting a ACV signal to the gauge, and the wiring connectors are in excellent condition making solid contact.But the gauge does not move, even after substituting another complete gauge and circit board.How can I test the meter coils and motors? 
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||05-30-99||01:20 AM||||How did you measure that it is geting a signal (since it's only getting a signal when you're driving)?
Z||000005||ICQ196344||05-30-99||07:29 AM||||Yes, I hooked my volt meter into the connector at the gauge and obtained a reading while driving down the road. In 4th gear the reading was 1.8vac at 1000rpm, 2.8 @ 1500 rpm, 3.8 @ 2000rpm, and 4.8 @ 2500 rpm.(The rpm readings are not correct since recalibrating the tach it appears to be reading about 50% higher than it should.)I also took a reading by driving the sensor with a electric drill and obtained an increasing voltage reading as I increased the drill speed.I have concluded the sensor, the wiring, and the connectors are all ok, and that the problem is in the circit board or the coils that drive the speedometer's needle. But since I replace the gauge with another without success I am not sure how to proceed.
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||05-30-99||02:27 PM||||Where did you get the ground for the voltmeter? It sounds like the print has no ground. Easiest way to measure that: Set your voltmeter to DC, put the + terminal of the voltmeter on a point with battery voltage and ground the voltmeter on the ground of the speedometer connector (disconnect it first!). If you don't read battery voltage, your ground wire is the problem.
Z||000007||ICQ196344||05-31-99||06:49 PM||||Cliff, <br>You were right! It turned out to be a lost ground to the meter. Now that I have both the tach and speedometer working, I need to work on calibrating them, because of the v-8 conversion I completed. I replaced the .01uF capacitor with one with a .oo68uF value in the tach as described in an old issue of the  Fiero Owner, but reading is still too high by about 50%.In the speedometer I repaced the crystal that was rated at 4.194MHz with one rated at 4.43MHz to increase the maximum speedometer reading to 120 MPH. Now the speedometer starts at 40 MPH instead to 0. Any idea what the proper ratings should be or how find the right readings? Thanks so much for your help finding the bad ground.<p>                BRIAN CORNELIUS<p><br>
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||06-01-99||01:20 AM||||Brian, I'm glad you found the problem!<p>On the tach: which capacitor did you replace (where on the print)? Or did you replace the tachfilter?<p>On the speedometer: I've never before heared of such a thing as replacing the crystal to get higher readings... Where did you get that from? I'll have to take a look at the original speedometer print again to see what actually needs to be done. I suspect you should replace the air-core driver instead (if anything needs to be replaced at all).
